WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.734
A secure RTIC system starts with an
architecture that another engineer can

2
00:00:03.814 --> 00:00:04.378
understand.

3
00:00:04.458 --> 00:00:07.230
Reviewers should be able to identify
initialization behavior,

4
00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:10.646
idle behavior, hardware-facing tasks,
communication tasks, timing tasks,

5
00:00:10.726 --> 00:00:12.317
shared resources, local resources,

6
00:00:12.397 --> 00:00:15.836
and safety-relevant actions without
reverse engineering the whole firmware.

7
00:00:15.916 --> 00:00:17.918
Initialization is part of the security
model.

8
00:00:17.998 --> 00:00:21.077
It decides which peripherals exist, which
resources are initialized,

9
00:00:21.157 --> 00:00:23.159
which tasks are enabled, what defaults
apply,

10
00:00:23.239 --> 00:00:25.241
and what happens if a resource is
unavailable.

11
00:00:25.321 --> 00:00:28.811
Idle behavior also matters because
low-power states, background maintenance,

12
00:00:28.891 --> 00:00:32.022
and watchdog interactions can affect
device reliability and recovery.

13
00:00:32.102 --> 00:00:34.771
RTIC resources should have clear ownership
and access rules.

14
00:00:34.851 --> 00:00:37.468
Local resources can keep state close to
the task that owns it.

15
00:00:37.548 --> 00:00:41.141
Shared resources should exist for a
specific reason and should not become a dumping

16
00:00:41.221 --> 00:00:42.299
ground for global state.

17
00:00:42.379 --> 00:00:46.023
The more broadly shared a resource is, the
more important its access policy becomes.

18
00:00:46.103 --> 00:00:48.567
Focused task responsibilities improve
reviewability.

19
00:00:48.647 --> 00:00:51.367
A single task should not casually mix
parsing, authorization,

20
00:00:51.447 --> 00:00:53.295
hardware control, logging, diagnostics,

21
00:00:53.375 --> 00:00:56.506
and recovery if those concerns need
separate timing or trust decisions.

22
00:00:56.586 --> 00:01:00.230
Clear separation helps reviewers
understand what input can influence which device

23
00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:00.772
behavior.

24
00:01:00.852 --> 00:01:03.675
Security review should map input paths to
resources and actions.

25
00:01:03.755 --> 00:01:06.013
When a message, sensor value, maintenance
command,

26
00:01:06.093 --> 00:01:09.481
or timer event enters the system,
reviewers should know which task handles it,

27
00:01:09.561 --> 00:01:12.127
what resource it can touch, what
validation happens first,

28
00:01:12.207 --> 00:01:14.260
and what failure behavior protects the
device.
